The '''History of Guajicutea (1555–1590)''' covers a period of relative peace under [[Dawit I of Guajicutea|Boqor Dawit I]], marked by population recovery, civic improvements, and the expansion of plantation agriculture fueled by enslaved [[Kaftians|Kaftian]] labor. The era was shaped by the growing rivalry between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]] for influence over the Guaji court, expressed through competing investments in the port cities of [[Waribá]] and [[Barikú]]. The [[Costabella Agreement]] of 1565 coordinated the two powers' respective conquests, while the establishment of the [[Kingdom of Murani]] in 1564 — a diplomatic initiative by Dawit I — created a second Christian protectorate in the Greater Capuyaquiran. The period's prosperity ended in crisis: as [[New Burawa]] stabilized with the capitulation of the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]] in 1575, demand for Guaji cash crops collapsed, triggering a depression in 1573 that led Dawit I to proclaim the end of slavery. In the final decade, both colonial powers turned their attention to their mainland and archipelagic territories, leaving Guajicutea with greater autonomy but less support. The era ended with the outbreak of the [[War of the Mesogean]] in 1590 — the first direct conflict between Burawa and Volisania.

== Population recovery ==

The island's population, reduced to approximately 25,000 after the Great Pestilence (1531–1538), began a steady recovery during this period. Peace, expanded agriculture, and trade supported consistent growth:

