After the abolition of slavery in 1573, the Kaftian population was no longer counted separately. By 1575, approximately 800 freed Kaftians were being assimilated into the general population.

Growth was concentrated in the three cities — [[Tabakurí]] (the inland capital), [[Waribá]] (the northeastern port), and [[Barikú]] (the western port) — and in the southern plantation districts. Rural areas recovered more slowly, limited by the feudal land system.

== The plantation economy ==

Southern Guajicutea's plantation economy, established in the 1540s with enslaved Kaftian labor, expanded during this period. Cash crops — primarily foodstuffs and export crops — were grown on large estates and sold to [[Burawa]].

[[New Burawa]], engaged in the [[Aruanãyára]] war (1547–1575), could not produce sufficient agricultural goods domestically. The wartime economy created a reliable market for Guaji plantation exports. [[Volisania]], with its own colonial production in the [[Balboa]] colony, was a smaller buyer.

The plantation economy enriched the southern [[bikabá|bikabás]] (feudal lords), who controlled the largest estates and the enslaved workforce. The [[Bukan]] bikabá in the northern highlands continued to control gold mine access, while the southern lords dominated the cash crop trade.

== The Guaji Peasant Rebellion (1571) ==

The expansion of slave-labor plantations created growing pressure on Guaji peasants. Free peasants could not compete with enslaved labor — plantations produced crops more cheaply, undercutting peasant farmers in the market. Peasants were pushed onto marginal land or forced into tenant labor on plantation estates.

By the late 1560s, the pressure had turned to open revolt. The [[Guaji Peasant Rebellion]] of 1571 saw peasants in the southern and central districts rise against plantation estates and the feudal lords who operated them. Rebels attacked plantation holdings, freed enslaved workers, and marched on local administrative centers. The rebellion was not directed at the king but at the southern bikabás and the slave economy they controlled.

Dawit I responded with a combination of force and concession. The rebellion was suppressed, but the king recognized the underlying grievance. In 1571, he banned the further import of enslaved Kaftians — a direct response to the rebellion. The ban did not free existing slaves but stopped the growth of the enslaved population through new imports.

== Economic depression and the end of slavery (1573) ==

As the Aruanãyára war approached its final phase, [[New Burawa]]'s own agricultural production began to recover. Burawi purchases of Guaji cash crops declined, and the plantation economy — built around the wartime market — contracted sharply. In 1573, the island entered an economic depression.

The depression made the plantation system unsustainable. With export markets shrinking and peasant discontent growing, Dawit I proclaimed the end of slavery. The enslaved Kaftian population — small, estimated at fewer than 800 — was freed. Dawit attempted to assimilate the Kaftians into Guaji society, integrating them as free laborers and peasants. The small size of the Kaftian population made assimilation feasible; they were too few to form a distinct community and were absorbed into the rural population over the following years.

== The Burawi-Volisania rivalry ==

With the island at peace, the competition between Burawa and Volisania shifted from military to economic and cultural influence. Both powers invested in their respective port cities — Burawa in [[Waribá]], Volisania in [[Barikú]] — building large plazas, market structures, guest houses, and amenities to court the king's favor. Dawit I, installed with Volisanian support but still a Burawi vassal, maintained dual loyalties, and both powers sought to pull him closer.

The rivalry was felt primarily in the port cities. [[Tabakurí]], the inland capital, remained the political center but was less touched by the colonial competition. The two ports became showcases of their respective colonial cultures — Waribá with its Burawi mission and administrative buildings, Barikú with its Volisanian fort and Petran Catholic church.

Volisania reduced its courting effort after the [[Costabella Agreement]] of 1565, having secured Burawa's cooperation for the Tapuya conquest. Burawa continued investing in Waribá, maintaining its presence as the senior colonial power on the island.

== The Costabella Agreement (1565) ==

In 1565, Burawa and Volisania signed the [[Costabella Agreement]] in [[Costabella]], the Volisanian capital of the [[Balboa]] colony. The agreement coordinated the two powers' colonial expansion:

* Volisania would aid Burawa in completing the conquest of the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]].
* Burawa would support Volisania's conquest of mainland [[Tapuya Confederacy|Tapuya]], beginning in 1568 and continuing until 1593.

The agreement temporarily reduced colonial competition over Guajicutea. Both powers had larger ambitions on the mainland, and cooperation served their interests better than continued rivalry over a small island. The agreement also formalized the division of colonial spheres: Burawa in the east and on the Aruanãyára front, Volisania in the west and on the Tapuya front.

== The Kingdom of Murani (1564) ==

In 1564, Dawit I arranged the establishment of the [[Kingdom of Murani]] — a Christian protectorate in the Greater Capuyaquiran, covering the three Murani islands (Maruba, Dukira, Waturi). The Murani had been conquered in the First Capuyaquiran War (1540–1543) and held as part of the Badweyn Islands under Guaji technical control but Burawi authority. Dawit I's deal elevated them from conquered tributaries to a protectorate kingdom with status similar to Guajicutea's.

The arrangement was secured through a marriage alliance: the [[Caracuran]] bikabári — whose family carried the maritime traditions of the Caracurí sea people and maintained trade connections to the eastern islands — married his daughters to the Murani king. The marriage tied Murani nobility to the Guaji court and gave Dawit I a diplomatic lever in the eastern archipelago.

The Burawi government had little interest in directly administering the small Murani islands, but the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] was eager for a new Christianization project. Dawit I convinced the church to support protectorate status rather than direct annexation, arguing that a Christianized Murani kingdom would serve Burawi interests better than another colonial possession. The Murani king was baptized and took a Burawi regnal name, becoming a Boqor under the Burawi church's authority.

== Civic and cultural life ==

The period saw improvements in civic life, particularly in the three cities. The competing investments of Burawa and Volisania produced new public spaces — plazas, markets, religious buildings, and improved infrastructure. Cultural life flourished in the urban centers, with trade bringing new goods, ideas, and people to the island.

However, outside the three cities, life remained largely unchanged. The feudal system established in the 1530s–1540s continued: [[bikabári|bikabáris]] (feudal lords) controlled land, peasants worked the fields, and mining communities extracted gold and stone. The civic improvements were an urban phenomenon, concentrated in the ports and the capital.

== Southern mining expansion (1570) ==

Gold production at the northern mines — [[Mina Yaruma]] on the eastern slopes of [[Cerro Caracurí]] — had been declining for years. Centuries of panning and surface mining had depleted the easily accessible deposits.

[[Monte Yukumá]], the great volcano in the south, had long been considered too sacred for mining. It was the source of the yukubara (volcanic mudflow) that buried [[Guajicutea Vieja]] in 516 CE, and the mountain's domain was forbidden to miners. But with northern gold drying up and economic pressure mounting, exploration began along the southern rivers of Monte Yukumá. In 1570, the first mines were opened, working placer gold from river gravels on the volcano's southern slopes.

The opening of the southern mines marked a break with a thousand-year taboo. The old religious authority that would have enforced the prohibition — the bohíques and priestesses — had been abolished under the colonial reforms. The decision was driven by economic necessity, not religious consensus.

== Capitulation of the Aruanãyára (1575) ==

The capitulation of the [[Aruanãyára Empire|Aruanãyára]] in 1575 marked the end of the Aruanãyára war and the stabilization of [[New Burawa]]. The end of the wartime market for Guaji exports, the abolition of slavery, and the economic depression of 1573 had already reshaped the island's economy.

== The Third Capuyaquiran War (1578–1581) ==

With the Aruanãyára war concluded, Burawa turned its attention to the remaining unincorporated islands of the Greater Capuyaquiran. The Third Capuyaquiran War (1578–1581) saw Burawa annex the last independent islands in the eastern archipelago. Only Guajicutea and the [[Kingdom of Murani]] were excluded — both held protectorate status under the Burawi church.

The war was brief and one-sided. The remaining islands lacked the military capacity to resist Burawi naval forces. By 1581, the eastern archipelago was fully divided between Burawa's colonial territories and the two protectorate kingdoms.

== Colonial withdrawal and Guaji autonomy ==

In the 1580s, both Burawa and Volisania began withdrawing attention from Guajicutea. With the Aruanãyára war won and the Tapuya conquest (1568–1593) nearing completion, both powers focused on consolidating and developing their own colonial territories across the Lesser and Greater Capuyaquiran. The competing investments in [[Waribá]] and [[Barikú]] slowed. Burawi military presence, already reduced after the civil war, drew down further. The Volisanian fort at Barikú maintained a skeleton garrison.

For Dawit I, the withdrawal brought greater autonomy but also reduced support. The island was left to manage its own affairs more independently than at any point since the establishment of the kingdom. Trade continued — Guaji gold, timber, and foodstuffs remained valuable — but the colonial powers were no longer courting the king with plazas and amenities.

In the 1580s, Dawit I began actively courting new foreign powers to diversify trade and reduce dependence on Burawa and Volisania. Ships from [[Mercia]], [[Breisland]], and [[Sarta]] arrived at Guaji ports for docking and trade. Cash crop exports, which had collapsed after the 1573 depression, recovered as new markets opened — particularly with Sarta, which became the largest new buyer. The diversification gave Dawit I economic leverage he had not possessed when Burawa and Volisania were the island's only trading partners.

The feudal system continued unchanged outside the three cities. The [[bikabári|bikabáris]] governed their territories, peasants worked the land, and the southern mines along Monte Yukumá's rivers expanded slowly. The Kaftian population, freed in 1573, was gradually absorbed into rural society.

== The souring of relations ==

By the late 1580s, the cooperation between Burawa and Volisania that had been formalized in the [[Costabella Agreement]] (1565) was breaking down. The two powers' respective conquests — Burawa's Aruanãyára campaign and Volisania's Tapuya campaign — were nearing completion, and the strategic logic of cooperation no longer held. Disputes over mainland territories, trade routes, and colonial spheres of influence grew increasingly bitter.

The souring was felt on Guajicutea as the two colonial powers began positioning themselves for a potential conflict. The reduced but continuing Burawi presence in Waribá and the Volisanian presence in Barikú took on a new significance — not as economic investments, but as strategic footholds. Dawit I, still nominally a Burawi vassal, found himself caught between two powers that were no longer allies.

== End of the era: the War of the Mesogean (1590) ==

In 1590, the tensions between Burawa and Volisania erupted into the [[War of the Mesogean]] — the first direct conflict between the two colonial powers. The war marked the end of the cooperative era that had begun with the Costabella Agreement and the beginning of a new period of colonial conflict that would reshape the Capuyaquiran and the mainland.

For Guajicutea, the outbreak of war meant the end of two decades of relative peace. The island's position between the two powers' spheres — Burawi influence in the east, Volisanian presence in the west — made neutrality difficult to maintain. The era that followed would test Dawit I's dual loyalties as never before.

